 for the orchestrator's checkpoint + `done` transition. Tree left byte-identical to `ebc4e31`, `git status` clean.\n\n## On `repository_delta=empty`\n\nNot an empty delivery. The CR's `base_oid` **is** the leaf commit and `candidate_tree_oid 49e6cb0d` equals both that commit's tree and HEAD's tree — a zero-width window over already-committed work. The delivery is the commit: `d1d3ece..ebc4e319` = 20 files, +3950/−37; the rework alone = 10 files, +317/−33. Reviewed both as the real change. Signature `Good \"git\" signature for oparin@me.com`, one commit past checkpoint. The snapshot machinery has now failed twice on this leaf for two different reasons (rev1 checkpoint move, rev2 handoff-after-amend) — orchestration defect, disclosed by the producer, flagged for a board item.\n\n## Both blocking findings closed, reproduced not accepted\n\n**F1 deep copy.** My own probe, 14 attacks across map-in-map / map-in-slice / slice-in-slice / slice-in-map at depths 1–3, inbound *and* outbound: encoded bytes unchanged on all of them, `Decode` of own output byte-identical. The copy is complete for the admitted set, and I attacked the one hole a Go type switch leaves — a **named** map type falling through `cloneDetailValue`'s `default` arm as a live alias. `axerror.Details` itself is such a type and is refused upstream. `Detail` deep-copies *per call* — noted as a cost surprise, not a defect.\n\n**F2 wrong actor.** Clause dropped, gap names #5 and #6 on the same reasoning, 5/7 and 9/394 consistent in all four published places. I re-read SPEC.md:11047 myself: its only RFC 2119 sentence binds the plugin.\n\n**No third wrong-actor clause.** Read every claimed line, named the actor its keyword governs — 11002/11003/11019 emitter, 11010 readers, 11053 receivers, 11121 reader, 11133 implementations. All actors this repo implements.\n\n## Attacked, not read\n\n41 mutations applied and compiled, **39 killed, 2 survivors**, preferring narrowings. I *discarded* one mutant as a compile failure rather than scoring it a kill — the producer's harness has no build gate and would have scored it KILLED. Re-ran the producer's 26 on a clean tree: 26 KILLED, 0 SURVIVED.\n\nTwo things worth naming beyond the brief: the digest pin **is** recomputed (one added full stop reddens it), and — more interesting — **the gate checks the claim, not only the pin**: pointing clause 15.1#1 at line 11004 with the digest blanked is refused with `declares line 11004, but the pinned clause is at line 11002`. A claim can't be relocated to a friendlier sentence even by someone who recomputes the pin.\n\nCoverage 99.7% **measured by me**, not accepted from the log. §15.2 table re-derived from SPEC by parser: every row matches.\n\n## Two non-blocking findings\n\n- **R1** — `minRedactableCause = 8` decides which causes get scanned at all, and raising it to 16 *or* 64 leaves the suite green; only 4096 reddens. The redaction gate's sensitivity bound is undefended by any test.\n- **R2** — `README.md:1248` and `registry.go:64`, both new here, call §15.2 the \"nineteen-row\" table. SPEC.md:11077–11094 has **eighteen** rows. Implementation and its reviewed literal are correct at 18; only the sentence is wrong. It repeats a miscount already in pre-existing repo prose, so fix at source.\n\n## Stated bounds\n\n`internal/axerror` still has **no production caller anywhere** — every behavior proven at the package boundary, none end-to-end. The clause scanner cannot decide which actor a MUST binds; nothing here closes that class and my actor table is a human read, not a machine check. No fuzz targets exist. I did not re-verify the 15-row binding table this round — `binding.go` is untouched by the rework, and that's an accepted round-1 result, not a round-2 measurement.","ttft_ms":1855,"type":"result","duration_ms":1040497,"uuid":"e8bbc29c-270e-4226-8406-6af9677a0e9c","ttft_stream_ms":1087,"time_to_request_ms":16,"queued_turn_count":0}
